WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.932
This is the technical deep dive for Book
02 of the Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.932 --> 00:00:09.809
Identity and Directory, for the
architects and engineers who

4
00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:13.687
will run the move from Active
Directory to Microsoft Entra ID.

5
00:00:13.687 --> 00:00:17.994
What Book 02 accomplishes is a
directory where placement has become

6
00:00:17.994 --> 00:00:22.526
attributes and rules, and every service
account has an owner and a target.

7
00:00:22.526 --> 00:00:24.668
Of the workbook's four tracks,

8
00:00:24.668 --> 00:00:29.846
it carries the directory side of the
HR track and the service accounts;

9
00:00:29.846 --> 00:00:35.738
people's attributes are Book 03, devices
and servers Book 05, and AI agents Book 17.

10
00:00:35.738 --> 00:00:40.137
Part 1 assesses the forest, starting
with what an ordinary account can see.

11
00:00:40.137 --> 00:00:43.700
Part 2 builds the Entra ID
target and moves onto it.

12
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.750
The code lives in the chapters.

13
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.630
I'll stick to what matters.

14
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:52.767
Here's why this matters.

15
00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:57.893
Federal policy moved the access decision
off the network and onto identity.

16
00:00:57.893 --> 00:01:04.106
OMB M-22-09, NIST's zero trust architecture
and CISA's maturity model all point

17
00:01:04.106 --> 00:01:09.242
the same way: no trust from network
location, and phishing-resistant sign-in.

18
00:01:09.242 --> 00:01:13.693
That makes the identity provider the policy
decision point for the whole estate.

19
00:01:13.693 --> 00:01:18.593
Whatever it believes about a person, a
group or a device gets enforced everywhere.

20
00:01:18.593 --> 00:01:23.591
Microsoft isn't retiring Active
Directory, but its investment is clearly

21
00:01:23.591 --> 00:01:28.754
moving to the cloud, from the sync engine
to sign-in to the deprecation of NTLM.

22
00:01:28.754 --> 00:01:30.817
The trap is the lift and shift.

23
00:01:30.817 --> 00:01:32.743
Sync the forest as it stands,

24
00:01:32.743 --> 00:01:36.931
and the stale accounts and
undocumented trusts come with it,

25
00:01:36.931 --> 00:01:42.650
while the intent the organizational unit
(OU) tree carried has nowhere to land.

26
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.025
So what actually has to move?

27
00:01:48.025 --> 00:01:50.625
Two points drive everything
in this session.

28
00:01:50.625 --> 00:01:53.237
First, placement becomes attributes.

29
00:01:53.237 --> 00:01:56.451
In Active Directory, where
an object sits in the OU

30
00:01:56.451 --> 00:01:59.900
tree decides who manages it
and which policy applies.

31
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:04.350
Entra ID has no tree; it governs
objects by what they carry.

32
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.115
Administrative units
help, but they can't nest,

33
00:02:07.115 --> 00:02:10.825
and for synced users the values
are still written on-premises.

34
00:02:10.825 --> 00:02:14.250
Second, the surface is bigger
than users and groups.

35
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:19.110
The inventory names eleven dependency
types, and some, like PKI,

36
00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:24.438
RADIUS and LDAP-bound applications,
fail silently when AD goes away.

37
00:02:24.438 --> 00:02:29.587
The workbook sorts what moves into four
tracks: people on the HR track, devices and

38
00:02:29.587 --> 00:02:34.737
servers on the Intune and Arc tracks, and
service accounts on the non-person track.

39
00:02:34.737 --> 00:02:39.710
The rest of this session is how you find
all of that, rebuild it, and prove it.

40
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:46.060
Module 1 is read-only discovery.

41
00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:50.175
An assessment team rarely gets privileged
access on day one, so the program

42
00:02:50.175 --> 00:02:54.822
spends that waiting time learning what the
directory already tells every employee.

43
00:02:54.822 --> 00:02:57.889
It changes nothing, and
it turns the request for

44
00:02:57.889 --> 00:03:01.400
elevated access into a
list built from evidence.

45
00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:08.362
It's surprising how much an
ordinary domain account can read.

46
00:03:08.362 --> 00:03:11.301
Topology, sites and domain controllers.

47
00:03:11.301 --> 00:03:13.649
The OU tree and its Group Policy links,

48
00:03:13.649 --> 00:03:17.287
which is the structure the
attribute design will replace.

49
00:03:17.287 --> 00:03:19.576
Most GPOs and their settings.

50
00:03:19.576 --> 00:03:23.196
Users, groups, computers
and service principal names,

51
00:03:23.196 --> 00:03:25.500
which is your identity inventory.

52
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.337
Even the certificate templates.

53
00:03:27.337 --> 00:03:31.899
What you usually can't see is replication
health and DNS server settings;

54
00:03:31.899 --> 00:03:33.875
those become a delegation item.

55
00:03:33.875 --> 00:03:37.763
The default is broad because of a
handful of built-in read grants,

56
00:03:37.763 --> 00:03:42.238
and exactly how broad depends on choices
made when the domain was created.

57
00:03:42.238 --> 00:03:46.415
So the first step is a pre-flight that
tests what this account can really

58
00:03:46.415 --> 00:03:51.240
read in this forest, and every later
export records which access it relied on.

59
00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:58.839
The assessment report should never
claim coverage it doesn't have,

60
00:03:58.839 --> 00:04:02.128
so you need to know exactly
where that read boundary sits.

61
00:04:02.128 --> 00:04:03.616
Four mechanisms set it.

62
00:04:03.616 --> 00:04:08.453
Protected accounts: the admin count flag
marks accounts that are or were privileged,

63
00:04:08.453 --> 00:04:12.877
which makes it a good clean-up list, but
not the current privileged population.

64
00:04:12.877 --> 00:04:16.951
Confidential attributes, like LAPS
passwords: you can't read them,

65
00:04:16.951 --> 00:04:21.628
but the presence and age of the expiration
attribute still shows coverage.

66
00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:26.602
Password secrets, which leave a domain
controller only through replication rights

67
00:04:26.602 --> 00:04:28.379
the assessment never asks for,

68
00:04:28.379 --> 00:04:31.790
though who already holds those
rights is a key later check.

69
00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.740
And restricted containers
like Deleted Objects.

70
00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.660
The rule that ties it together is simple:
report only what you actually measured.

71
00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:48.348
Every gap that read-only discovery couldn't
close gets written down: what's missing,

72
00:04:48.348 --> 00:04:51.034
the narrowest right or
export that closes it,

73
00:04:51.034 --> 00:04:54.810
why the plan needs it, and whether
there's a read-only substitute.

74
00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.883
Some come as exports from
the right administrator,

75
00:04:57.883 --> 00:05:01.835
like replication health or the
CA's issued-certificate database.

76
00:05:01.835 --> 00:05:06.460
Some, like LAPS and BitLocker recovery
data, are never requested at all.

77
00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:11.366
The gap list becomes a short written
request with firm rules: read only,

78
00:05:11.366 --> 00:05:14.495
specific to an object
and a reason, time-bound,

79
00:05:14.495 --> 00:05:18.048
one named account, and an
export wherever possible.

80
00:05:18.048 --> 00:05:21.635
Domain Admin for the assessment
is not an acceptable line.

81
00:05:21.635 --> 00:05:22.873
And run it safely.

82
00:05:22.873 --> 00:05:27.449
Tell security operations first, because
broad directory queries look like

83
00:05:27.449 --> 00:05:32.450
reconnaissance, and keep the output
encrypted, because it's a map of the estate.

84
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:39.312
Module 2 is the full
assessment of the forest.

85
00:05:39.312 --> 00:05:44.013
Chapter 08 fills the gap list with
the privileged half of discovery.

86
00:05:44.013 --> 00:05:49.255
The program's position is that the assessment
produces evidence, not just script

87
00:05:49.255 --> 00:05:54.310
output: fixed artifacts, a hashed
manifest, and every run committed to Git.

88
00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:01.502
The full assessment covers
eight areas in a fixed order,

89
00:06:01.502 --> 00:06:03.972
from forest topology through to trusts.

90
00:06:03.972 --> 00:06:07.641
Most of the forest is readable
by any authenticated user,

91
00:06:07.641 --> 00:06:11.309
so each collector runs on the
narrowest right that works,

92
00:06:11.309 --> 00:06:15.836
like read on the OU security descriptors,
or read on the PKI containers.

93
00:06:15.836 --> 00:06:18.785
Two rights the source design
asked for are refused.

94
00:06:18.785 --> 00:06:23.035
DnsAdmins membership, because
Microsoft treats it as privileged.

95
00:06:23.035 --> 00:06:26.247
And standing Domain Admin,
because no run needs it.

96
00:06:26.247 --> 00:06:29.309
Trust verification is
the only step that does,

97
00:06:29.309 --> 00:06:32.773
and it runs separately under
just-in-time elevation.

98
00:06:32.773 --> 00:06:38.073
Collection runs from a domain-joined
Windows Server 2025 or Windows 11 host,

99
00:06:38.073 --> 00:06:42.785
under a group managed service account,
so no password is ever stored.

100
00:06:42.785 --> 00:06:46.810
Next, the three areas that carry
most of the planning weight.

101
00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.147
Three areas carry most
of the planning weight.

102
00:06:53.147 --> 00:06:54.548
Topology first.

103
00:06:54.548 --> 00:06:59.637
Record every operations master role,
and remember there are three per domain

104
00:06:59.637 --> 00:07:04.647
plus two for the forest, so a three-domain
forest has eleven to record, not five.

105
00:07:04.647 --> 00:07:08.497
Flag any functional level
below Windows Server 2016,

106
00:07:08.497 --> 00:07:12.085
because it blocks the
domain controller refresh.

107
00:07:12.085 --> 00:07:13.873
Second, the OU tree.

108
00:07:13.873 --> 00:07:16.409
The goal is to capture what it encodes,

109
00:07:16.409 --> 00:07:20.336
so the attribute design
reproduces intent, not structure.

110
00:07:20.336 --> 00:07:24.985
The delegations someone made by hand
become the administrative unit candidates.

111
00:07:24.985 --> 00:07:28.360
Third, Group Policy, the biggest artifact.

112
00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:32.502
Every live setting moves to Intune,
moves elsewhere, or retires,

113
00:07:32.502 --> 00:07:35.928
and overlapping settings
have to be resolved first,

114
00:07:35.928 --> 00:07:40.030
because Intune's migration
refuses conflicting values.

115
00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.530
AD CS matters twice in a migration.

116
00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:51.645
It issues the certificates devices, domain
controllers and smart cards rely on,

117
00:07:51.645 --> 00:07:54.590
and its misconfigurations
are among the most

118
00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:57.768
direct paths to privilege
escalation in a forest.

119
00:07:57.768 --> 00:08:03.340
The assessment checks the known patterns it
can see, the ESC family from SpecterOps'

120
00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:08.267
2021 research, with Defender for
Identity's definitions as the reference.

121
00:08:08.267 --> 00:08:12.603
The correction that matters most:
the source's ESC1 check looked for

122
00:08:12.603 --> 00:08:17.093
a key usage almost every template
has, so it flagged nearly everything.

123
00:08:17.093 --> 00:08:21.694
The real test is whether the template
allows an authentication purpose, and a

124
00:08:21.694 --> 00:08:26.367
template only counts if a CA actually
publishes it and broad groups can enroll.

125
00:08:26.367 --> 00:08:27.768
This is report only.

126
00:08:27.768 --> 00:08:32.650
Fixing it belongs to the
PKI owner, through Book 07.

127
00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:40.632
Every computer object is a
migration decision: Entra join,

128
00:08:40.632 --> 00:08:44.775
hybrid join, Azure Arc, retire,
or keep in Active Directory.

129
00:08:44.775 --> 00:08:49.812
Delegation is where the source got things
wrong, so classify by what's actually set.

130
00:08:49.812 --> 00:08:55.163
Unconstrained delegation lets a service
impersonate any user to any service.

131
00:08:55.163 --> 00:08:59.837
Domain controllers are expected to have
it; anywhere else, it's a critical issue.

132
00:08:59.837 --> 00:09:05.096
Treat a computer as stale only when both
its last logon and its password are old,

133
00:09:05.096 --> 00:09:08.250
because the logon date can
lag by up to two weeks.

134
00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:11.597
For users, expand the
privileged groups recursively,

135
00:09:11.597 --> 00:09:13.575
so nested members aren't missed.

136
00:09:13.575 --> 00:09:17.712
And among service accounts, user
accounts with service principal names

137
00:09:17.712 --> 00:09:22.080
are Kerberoastable, which makes them
the highest priority to replace.

138
00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:29.442
Trusts are where undocumented
history usually hides.

139
00:09:29.442 --> 00:09:34.971
The trust attributes are bit flags, so decode
them from the published specification,

140
00:09:34.971 --> 00:09:39.631
not from memory, and keep the raw value
too, so bits added later aren't lost.

141
00:09:39.631 --> 00:09:43.769
Then state direction plainly,
because it's easy to get backwards:

142
00:09:43.769 --> 00:09:46.268
access flows the opposite way to trust.

143
00:09:46.268 --> 00:09:48.293
The source design had it reversed.

144
00:09:48.293 --> 00:09:51.510
So for each trust, the
assessment says in plain words

145
00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:54.654
which side's users can reach
which side's resources.

146
00:09:54.654 --> 00:09:59.780
Selective authentication and SID
filtering are posture, not findings.

147
00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:05.630
Verification needs administrative credentials,
so it runs as its own elevated step.

148
00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:09.917
And a forest trust doesn't
convert into anything in Entra ID.

149
00:10:09.917 --> 00:10:15.210
It marks a partner whose access has to
be redesigned, which Module 4 covers.

150
00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:22.485
What makes a run evidence is the
pipeline around the collectors.

151
00:10:22.485 --> 00:10:27.195
Every run writes a manifest with a
SHA-256 hash of every output file,

152
00:10:27.195 --> 00:10:31.305
and a failed step is recorded
rather than stopping the run,

153
00:10:31.305 --> 00:10:33.960
so a partial run is visible as partial.

154
00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:36.874
Anyone holding the
repository can recompute the

155
00:10:36.874 --> 00:10:39.860
hashes and show nothing
changed after collection.

156
00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:44.885
Each run becomes a single commit on its
own branch, not hundreds of commits.

157
00:10:44.885 --> 00:10:48.285
Issues are raised automatically
for only two classes,

158
00:10:48.285 --> 00:10:53.725
critical PKI patterns and unconstrained
delegation outside the domain controllers;

159
00:10:53.725 --> 00:10:56.672
everything else feeds
the planning documents.

160
00:10:56.672 --> 00:11:00.202
Run it weekly, and compare
objects by a stable key,

161
00:11:00.202 --> 00:11:02.761
not timestamps, to see what drifted.

162
00:11:02.761 --> 00:11:06.950
And handle the data carefully,
because it's a map for an attacker.

163
00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:14.409
Part 2 builds the Entra ID
target and migrates onto it,

164
00:11:14.409 --> 00:11:17.275
starting with Module 3, the target design.

165
00:11:17.275 --> 00:11:22.540
The premise fits in one sentence: in
Active Directory, where an object sits

166
00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:28.520
decides what applies to it; in Entra ID,
what an object says about itself decides.

167
00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:36.056
Before any object moves, break
the AD model into its parts,

168
00:11:36.056 --> 00:11:38.795
because each lands somewhere different.

169
00:11:38.795 --> 00:11:44.141
OUs do three jobs: policy scoping becomes
Conditional Access and Intune profiles,

170
00:11:44.141 --> 00:11:46.971
delegation becomes administrative units,

171
00:11:46.971 --> 00:11:50.745
and organization becomes
dynamic groups and attributes.

172
00:11:50.745 --> 00:11:54.909
The forest becomes the tenant, trusts
become cross-tenant access, and

173
00:11:54.909 --> 00:11:57.905
AdminSDHolder's purpose
is served by Privileged

174
00:11:57.905 --> 00:12:00.608
Identity Management and protected actions.

175
00:12:00.608 --> 00:12:04.232
The design principle is that
Entra ID inverts the model.

176
00:12:04.232 --> 00:12:07.991
Attributes decide memberships,
and memberships decide policy,

177
00:12:07.991 --> 00:12:11.395
which is why attribute quality
gates everything after it.

178
00:12:11.395 --> 00:12:15.345
Licensing gates the governance
features, and the slide has the map.

179
00:12:15.345 --> 00:12:20.545
One date to act on: the preview
member-of operator ends November 3,

180
00:12:20.545 --> 00:12:24.140
2026, so replace any
rules that use it now.

181
00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:31.217
Since placement becomes
attributes, it's worth seeing the

182
00:12:31.217 --> 00:12:34.752
attributes your team already
knows and what each one becomes.

183
00:12:34.752 --> 00:12:39.952
Most carry over, but often read-only,
and some can't be read by a rule at all.

184
00:12:39.952 --> 00:12:43.304
distinguishedName comes across,
but never use it for policy,

185
00:12:43.304 --> 00:12:45.515
because it's the placement we're leaving.

186
00:12:45.515 --> 00:12:50.615
Rules can't read manager or employeeType,
so they read coded copies instead.

187
00:12:50.615 --> 00:12:55.590
The disabled bit becomes accountEnabled,
and every access rule should require it.

188
00:12:55.590 --> 00:13:00.603
altSecurityIdentities can feed the PIV
binding, through sync rules you add.

189
00:13:00.603 --> 00:13:05.247
And two have no attribute at all:
Kerberos service principal names stay

190
00:13:05.247 --> 00:13:09.890
in AD until the workloads move, and
admin count gives way to roles and PIM.

191
00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:13.450
Next, the sync engine that
carries these values.

192
00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:20.287
The first hybrid-period
decision is the sync engine.

193
00:13:20.287 --> 00:13:25.200
Connect Sync is the long-standing,
full-featured engine on a Windows Server.

194
00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:28.862
Cloud Sync is a lightweight
agent configured from the cloud,

195
00:13:28.862 --> 00:13:32.012
and Microsoft now calls it
the strategic direction.

196
00:13:32.012 --> 00:13:34.225
The slide compares them side by side.

197
00:13:34.225 --> 00:13:39.526
The short version: Cloud Sync runs multiple
active agents with automatic failover,

198
00:13:39.526 --> 00:13:43.213
while Connect Sync still wins
on scale and advanced rules.

199
00:13:43.213 --> 00:13:46.776
Cloud Sync now covers group
provisioning back to AD and

200
00:13:46.776 --> 00:13:50.650
Exchange hybrid writeback, so
those no longer force Connect.

201
00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:53.678
The program's rule is Cloud
Sync for new deployments,

202
00:13:53.678 --> 00:13:57.550
and Connect only where a forest
needs something Cloud Sync can't do.

203
00:13:57.550 --> 00:14:02.330
Both can run in one forest with separate
scopes, so you can move in stages.

204
00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:08.905
The second decision is
where passwords are checked.

205
00:14:08.905 --> 00:14:12.001
With password hash sync,
it happens in the cloud,

206
00:14:12.001 --> 00:14:16.368
needs no extra infrastructure, and
survives an on-premises outage.

207
00:14:16.368 --> 00:14:20.288
Pass-through authentication
depends on on-premises agents,

208
00:14:20.288 --> 00:14:23.581
and federation with AD FS
needs whole server farms.

209
00:14:23.581 --> 00:14:28.204
Microsoft recommends turning on password
hash sync whichever method you choose,

210
00:14:28.204 --> 00:14:31.469
because it's the fallback, and
organizations that had it on

211
00:14:31.469 --> 00:14:35.005
recovered from ransomware outages
in hours rather than weeks.

212
00:14:35.005 --> 00:14:40.155
So the program prefers it everywhere,
and AD FS becomes a decommission target.

213
00:14:40.155 --> 00:14:41.417
Then, matching.

214
00:14:41.417 --> 00:14:45.391
When sync meets an object that
already exists in the cloud,

215
00:14:45.391 --> 00:14:48.868
a match overwrites the cloud
copy, so clean AD first.

216
00:14:48.868 --> 00:14:53.181
And since July 1, 2026, sync can't
hard-match onto cloud accounts

217
00:14:53.181 --> 00:14:57.930
with privileged roles, so don't
plan to take over admins that way.

218
00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:05.980
Groups and service accounts each get
one recorded decision per object.

219
00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:10.243
Every security group gets one of
five outcomes: keep it in AD,

220
00:15:10.243 --> 00:15:14.505
sync it, convert it to cloud-only,
make it dynamic, or retire it.

221
00:15:14.505 --> 00:15:18.920
Nesting works for fewer things in
Entra ID, since app assignment and

222
00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:23.493
licensing don't cascade, so flatten
deep chains and record the mapping.

223
00:15:23.493 --> 00:15:27.317
Service accounts are the
non-person track: no HR record,

224
00:15:27.317 --> 00:15:32.755
so each needs an owner and a target, and
the rule is to eliminate stored secrets.

225
00:15:32.755 --> 00:15:37.445
Accounts that call cloud APIs become
app registrations with certificates or

226
00:15:37.445 --> 00:15:41.342
federated credentials, Azure
workloads get managed identities,

227
00:15:41.342 --> 00:15:45.743
and on-premises Kerberos workloads go
to group managed service accounts.

228
00:15:45.743 --> 00:15:50.410
The application owner decides each
conversion, every replacement app gets

229
00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:55.280
two human owners, and an account nobody
will own is a retirement candidate.

230
00:15:59.030 --> 00:16:02.992
Privileged access has one core
rule: no standing access.

231
00:16:02.992 --> 00:16:07.322
Every privileged assignment is
eligible and activated just in time,

232
00:16:07.322 --> 00:16:11.043
and the only permanent ones
are the two emergency accounts.

233
00:16:11.043 --> 00:16:15.402
Domain Admins maps to Global
Administrator, held by very few,

234
00:16:15.402 --> 00:16:18.542
and most of its duties
map to narrower roles.

235
00:16:18.542 --> 00:16:20.484
The slide has the full mapping,

236
00:16:20.484 --> 00:16:23.980
which is the program's design,
not an exact equivalence.

237
00:16:23.980 --> 00:16:27.558
Activation is short, four hours
at most for the top roles,

238
00:16:27.558 --> 00:16:32.380
with approval and a ticket, and it forces
a fresh phishing-resistant sign-in.

239
00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:35.276
Name approvers outside
the requester's chain,

240
00:16:35.276 --> 00:16:38.630
because with none configured,
PIM can lock the tenant.

241
00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:43.748
The emergency accounts are cloud-only,
excluded from restrictive policies,

242
00:16:43.748 --> 00:16:48.260
alerted on every sign-in, and
tested at least every ninety days.

243
00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:55.459
Conditional Access replaces
the logon restrictions

244
00:16:55.459 --> 00:16:58.985
and network controls AD
enforced through Group Policy.

245
00:16:58.985 --> 00:17:01.895
The program organizes it around personas,

246
00:17:01.895 --> 00:17:06.552
populations with different needs
and risks: internal staff, admins,

247
00:17:06.552 --> 00:17:10.710
partners, guests, service accounts,
and the break-glass group.

248
00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:12.756
One tip: target admins by group,

249
00:17:12.756 --> 00:17:16.848
because role targeting doesn't
cover scoped or custom roles.

250
00:17:16.848 --> 00:17:21.842
The baseline set, CA001 through
CA017, runs from blocking legacy

251
00:17:21.842 --> 00:17:27.685
sign-in for everyone to requiring
phishing-resistant strength for admins.

252
00:17:27.685 --> 00:17:33.210
Book 04's library is the authoritative
set and extends it for the CISA baseline.

253
00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:36.366
Rollout is staged:
report-only, then a pilot,

254
00:17:36.366 --> 00:17:39.973
then phased waves, each
behind a monitoring gate.

255
00:17:39.973 --> 00:17:43.372
And never switch an all-users
policy straight from off to

256
00:17:43.372 --> 00:17:47.410
on without confirming both
emergency accounts still sign in.

257
00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:54.810
Module 4 is migrating,
governing and proving it.

258
00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:57.000
Chapter 10 states the finish line:

259
00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:00.285
the migration ends when
governance runs on its own.

260
00:18:00.285 --> 00:18:04.124
Joiners, movers and leavers
flow from HR without a ticket,

261
00:18:04.124 --> 00:18:07.830
and drift shows up in a diff
instead of an incident.

262
00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:15.027
Governance in Active Directory
was mostly a ticket queue:

263
00:18:15.027 --> 00:18:18.905
someone asked for access, someone
added it, and nobody took it away.

264
00:18:18.905 --> 00:18:21.093
The target replaces that with a loop.

265
00:18:21.093 --> 00:18:26.413
HR-driven provisioning creates the account,
Lifecycle Workflows run the joiner,

266
00:18:26.413 --> 00:18:30.860
mover and leaver tasks, access
packages grant access with an expiry,

267
00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:34.354
and access reviews remove
what's no longer justified.

268
00:18:34.354 --> 00:18:39.168
For example, seven days before the hire
date the manager is emailed a Temporary

269
00:18:39.168 --> 00:18:44.130
Access Pass, so the new hire registers a
phishing-resistant credential on day one.

270
00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:48.417
On the leave date, the account is
disabled and its sessions revoked.

271
00:18:48.417 --> 00:18:52.893
Two cautions: remove privileged
access before the delete task runs,

272
00:18:52.893 --> 00:18:56.968
and delete synced users in AD, or
they come back on the next sync.

273
00:18:56.968 --> 00:18:59.490
Quarterly reviews close the loop.

274
00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:06.965
Authentication modernization starts
from the federal requirement.

275
00:19:06.965 --> 00:19:11.301
OMB M-22-09 requires
phishing-resistant MFA,

276
00:19:11.301 --> 00:19:16.190
and an end to methods like SMS and
voice codes for routine access.

277
00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:19.830
Its targets were set for
the end of fiscal year 2024,

278
00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:22.690
so these are requirements now, not goals.

279
00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:24.440
That reorders the roadmap.

280
00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:26.603
Older plans put smart cards last,

281
00:19:26.603 --> 00:19:30.369
but a federal workforce already
carries PIV or CAC cards,

282
00:19:30.369 --> 00:19:35.977
so certificate-based authentication straight
against Entra ID is the fastest path.

283
00:19:35.977 --> 00:19:41.423
The program goes in four phases: privileged
accounts first, then every card holder,

284
00:19:41.423 --> 00:19:46.628
then managed Windows devices on Windows
Hello, then everyone else on passkeys.

285
00:19:46.628 --> 00:19:50.455
Then the legacy cutoff: find
the remaining legacy sign-ins,

286
00:19:50.455 --> 00:19:55.210
move them, block them, and watch for
thirty days until there are none.

287
00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:02.466
The migration runs in eleven
phases, zero through ten,

288
00:20:02.466 --> 00:20:07.485
and each one starts only when the previous
one's validation criteria are met.

289
00:20:07.485 --> 00:20:10.260
Every phase also has a rollback procedure.

290
00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:14.062
The durations are planning
estimates, not measured results,

291
00:20:14.062 --> 00:20:16.648
and the total runs forty to seventy weeks.

292
00:20:16.648 --> 00:20:19.958
It starts with the assessment,
then the sync deployment,

293
00:20:19.958 --> 00:20:22.836
with object counts matching
within one percent.

294
00:20:22.836 --> 00:20:25.709
Then sign-in, groups,
service accounts and PIM,

295
00:20:25.709 --> 00:20:30.835
which ends with no permanent admin
assignments except the emergency accounts.

296
00:20:30.835 --> 00:20:34.215
Conditional Access is
enforced only after MFA

297
00:20:34.215 --> 00:20:38.635
registration passes ninety-five
percent in each population.

298
00:20:38.635 --> 00:20:43.629
Then come the phishing-resistant roadmap,
the legacy cutoff, retiring federation,

299
00:20:43.629 --> 00:20:49.060
and finally steady-state governance, with
the operations runbook committed to Git.

300
00:20:52.810 --> 00:20:58.572
Proving it starts with logs, because Entra
ID keeps them only briefly: seven days

301
00:20:58.572 --> 00:21:03.898
on the free tier, thirty on P1 or P2,
and upgrading doesn't recover older data.

302
00:21:03.898 --> 00:21:07.503
So stream everything to Log
Analytics and keep sign-in

303
00:21:07.503 --> 00:21:10.709
and audit logs for three
hundred sixty-five days.

304
00:21:10.709 --> 00:21:15.048
On top of that, scheduled detections
watch for things like stale accounts,

305
00:21:15.048 --> 00:21:19.173
late-night privileged activations,
and any emergency account sign-in.

306
00:21:19.173 --> 00:21:24.403
For compliance, the controls map to
FedRAMP 20x Key Security Indicators,

307
00:21:24.403 --> 00:21:28.735
evidenced by the workflows, reviews
and PIM you've already built.

308
00:21:28.735 --> 00:21:30.372
And drift lives in Git.

309
00:21:30.372 --> 00:21:34.867
A weekly scan compares against a baseline
and opens an issue with the diff,

310
00:21:34.867 --> 00:21:38.772
and each finding is fixed at the
source, never by editing the copy.

311
00:21:38.772 --> 00:21:43.720
Today that covers user additions
and removals; the rest is planned.

312
00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:51.495
Chapter 11 puts it all together
on one representative estate.

313
00:21:51.495 --> 00:21:56.057
It's a composite, not a real engagement,
and the counts are illustrative.

314
00:21:56.057 --> 00:22:00.218
Picture about fifty thousand
users in two forests,

315
00:22:00.218 --> 00:22:06.657
joined by a forest trust nobody documented
or owns, moving to Microsoft 365 GCC.

316
00:22:06.657 --> 00:22:09.424
There are five phases, and
each ends with a signed

317
00:22:09.424 --> 00:22:12.121
readiness bundle that
the next one starts from.

318
00:22:12.121 --> 00:22:17.361
Discovery finds about four hundred
accounts with service principal names, and

319
00:22:17.361 --> 00:22:22.760
every SQL Server one is captured, because
a missing one silently falls back to NTLM.

320
00:22:22.760 --> 00:22:26.572
Normalizing resolves a clash
between two Operations OUs

321
00:22:26.572 --> 00:22:30.145
in an organizational registry,
not by renaming groups.

322
00:22:30.145 --> 00:22:33.558
Mapping finds four adapters
that carry the risk,

323
00:22:33.558 --> 00:22:36.970
including PKI and RADIUS,
which move in lockstep.

324
00:22:36.970 --> 00:22:41.550
Then migration and validation,
with every operation recorded.

325
00:22:45.300 --> 00:22:47.213
Last, what all this produces.

326
00:22:47.213 --> 00:22:51.248
During migration, the program
classifies drift into five kinds,

327
00:22:51.248 --> 00:22:55.588
and it's honest about maturity: two
are detected automatically today,

328
00:22:55.588 --> 00:22:59.700
one partly, and two still rely on
people reviewing the assessment.

329
00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:04.860
From Phase 2 on, the agency can produce
an evidence bundle at any time, with the

330
00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:07.563
readiness bundles, the SPN inventories,

331
00:23:07.563 --> 00:23:10.675
the provenance chain
and every drift finding.

332
00:23:10.675 --> 00:23:14.941
It goes to the security control
assessor and the authorizing official

333
00:23:14.941 --> 00:23:18.700
as the work happens, not assembled
over most of a year afterward.

334
00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:20.613
And it belongs to the agency.

335
00:23:20.613 --> 00:23:23.550
When the integrator
leaves, the evidence stays.

336
00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:29.155
So, from Monday: start read-only
discovery, keep the access request narrow,

337
00:23:29.155 --> 00:23:32.612
make every run evidence,
and gate every phase.

338
00:23:32.612 --> 00:23:36.837
Book 03, HR-Driven
Identity, picks up next.

339
00:23:36.837 --> 00:23:37.670
Thank you.
